PHASE 9 — SEARCH & CONTENT DISCOVERY

==================================================
PRIMARY GOAL
==================================================

A student should be able to search for a topic quickly.

Examples:

"decision tree"

"linear regression"

"svm"

"pca"

"machine learning"

"deep learning"

"bayesian"

"2 mark decision tree"

"10 mark svm"

Search should work across ALL available notes.

==================================================
IMPORTANT
==================================================

Do NOT build an external search service.

Do NOT add Algolia.

Do NOT add Elasticsearch.

Do NOT add a database just for search.

Start with a lightweight application-level search architecture that can scale to the current content size.

The search implementation should be designed so a future search engine can replace the search index without changing the UI.

==================================================
PART 1 — SEARCH ARCHITECTURE
==================================================

First inspect the existing note/content model.

Create a normalized searchable document for every topic.

Example:

{
  slug,
  title,
  description,
  course,
  branch,
  semester,
  subject,
  unit,
  tags,
  content,
  url
}

Do not duplicate the entire Markdown unnecessarily if a searchable plain-text representation can be generated.

==================================================
PART 2 — SEARCH INDEX
==================================================

Create a centralized search index.

The index should contain every valid topic.

Example:

searchIndex = [
  {
    title: "Linear Regression",
    description: "...",
    course: "BTech",
    branch: "CSE",
    subject: "Machine Learning",
    unit: 1,
    tags: [...],
    url: "/notes/btech/cse/machine-learning/linear-regression"
  }
]

The index must be generated from the existing source of truth.

DO NOT manually maintain a second list of notes.

==================================================
PART 3 — SEARCHABLE FIELDS
==================================================

Search should consider:

HIGH PRIORITY:

- title
- subject

MEDIUM PRIORITY:

- unit
- tags
- description

LOWER PRIORITY:

- body content

Do not treat every word equally.

Title matches should rank higher than body-content matches.

==================================================
PART 4 — SEARCH RANKING
==================================================

Implement simple deterministic relevance scoring.

Suggested priority:

Exact title match
        ↓
Title starts with query
        ↓
Title contains query
        ↓
Subject contains query
        ↓
Tags contain query
        ↓
Description contains query
        ↓
Body content contains query

Example:

Query:
"decision tree"

Result order:

1. Decision Tree
2. Decision Tree Algorithm
3. Random Forest
4. Machine Learning

Do not return random ordering.

==================================================
PART 5 — SEARCH UI
==================================================

Add a prominent search interface to:

/notes

Use the existing design language.

Do NOT redesign the entire page.

Search input should include:

Placeholder:

"Search notes, subjects, or topics..."

Support:

- typing
- Enter
- clear button
- result count

Example:

Search results for "decision tree"

4 notes found

==================================================
PART 6 — SEARCH RESULTS
==================================================

Each result should display:

Topic title

Short description

Course

Branch

Subject

Unit

optional tags

Example:

Decision Trees

BTech · CSE · Machine Learning
Unit 2

Decision trees are supervised learning models...

[Read Note]

The result itself should link to the canonical topic URL.

==================================================
PART 7 — EMPTY SEARCH
==================================================

When search input is empty:

Show the normal /notes content.

Do not show an empty search-results screen.

==================================================
PART 8 — NO RESULTS
==================================================

For no results:

Show:

"No notes found"

Then suggest alternatives.

Example:

No notes found for:

"decision neural network"

Try:

"decision tree"

"neural network"

"machine learning"

Do NOT invent suggestions.

Suggestions should come from actual indexed titles/subjects/tags where practical.

==================================================
PART 9 — FILTERS
==================================================

Add lightweight filters after basic search works.

Filters:

Course:
- All
- BTech
- MTech

Branch:
- All
- available branches

Subject:
- All
- available subjects

Semester:
- All
- available semesters

Do not show filters with zero available values.

==================================================
FILTER BEHAVIOR
==================================================

Filters must combine with search.

Example:

Query:
"machine"

Course:
BTech

Subject:
Machine Learning

Results should only contain matching BTech Machine Learning topics.

==================================================
PART 10 — URL STATE
==================================================

Search state should be shareable.

Use query parameters where appropriate.

Example:

/notes?q=decision+tree

Filters:

/notes?q=machine&course=btech&subject=machine-learning

Canonical URL MUST NOT include search query parameters.

Search pages should NOT automatically become indexable SEO landing pages.

==================================================
PART 11 — SEARCH SEO
==================================================

Do NOT create thousands of indexable search URLs.

Search result pages should normally use:

noindex

when a query parameter is present.

Canonical should point to:

/notes

Do not add search queries to sitemap.xml.

==================================================
PART 12 — PERFORMANCE
==================================================

Search must feel instant for the current dataset.

Avoid:

- network request on every keystroke
- API call on every character
- loading the full Markdown body into the browser unnecessarily

Use debouncing only if needed.

For the current dataset, client-side search is acceptable.

But structure the code so it can later move to server-side/search-engine search.

==================================================
PART 13 — MOBILE
==================================================

Search must work well on mobile.

Check:

- input width
- filter controls
- result cards
- horizontal overflow
- touch targets

Filters should not make the page difficult to use.

==================================================
PART 14 — KEYBOARD UX
==================================================

Support:

- Enter
- Escape to clear/close suggestions if autocomplete exists
- keyboard focus

Do not implement complex autocomplete unless it improves the current UX.

==================================================
PART 15 — SEARCH ANALYTICS PREPARATION
==================================================

Do not add Google Analytics in this phase.

However, structure search code so future events could capture:

search query
result count
selected result

Do not store personal user data.

==================================================
PART 16 — ACCESSIBILITY
==================================================

Search input must have:

- accessible label
- keyboard focus
- visible focus state
- appropriate aria attributes

If live result count is dynamically updated:

use an appropriate aria-live region.

Do not use placeholder text as the only accessible label.

==================================================
PART 17 — RESULT LIMIT
==================================================

Do not dump hundreds of results immediately.

Use a reasonable initial result limit.

Example:

20 results

If more exist:

Show:

"Load more"

or pagination.

Prefer Load More for this product.

==================================================
PART 18 — SEARCH INDEX VALIDATION
==================================================

Before finishing:

Compare the search index against the source note inventory.

Verify:

Every valid topic is searchable.

No nonexistent topic is searchable.

No duplicate topic entries.

No broken topic URLs.

